00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:46.070
are three typical steps that
we see from our customers

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:47.420
how they transition to cloud.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:50.550
So first step is called
hybrid infrastructure.

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:52.890
So that's when your
control is still on-prem,

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:54.770
and you're just getting
started with cloud.

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:56.900
Typically what we see is
different bursting scenarios.

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:58.983
You're just bursting your
computer into the cloud,

00:01:58.983 --> 00:02:00.770
or you're archiving
data to the cloud.

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:02.090
You're just getting started.

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:04.250
And what we see with Cloud
Storage in those cases,

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:06.510
typically, it's different
archival, different backup

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:08.630
scenarios, and customers
will be leveraging

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:12.190
our core compute, core storage,
and networking infrastructure.

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:14.940
And going a level deeper, the
next stage in the cloud journey

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:16.652
is typically lift and shift.

00:02:16.652 --> 00:02:18.110
You're just lifting
your workloads,

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:20.780
moving them to the cloud without
really changing that much.

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:23.390
And some typical examples
is moving your databases,

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:26.270
like SQL databases,
moving your Hadoop.

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:28.610
And then you'll end up
leveraging some of our existing

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:30.110
products, like
Google Cloud Storage,

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:32.540
or storing that Hadoop
data, or storing

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:34.190
different static content.

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:36.260
And you'll be relying
on Google Cloud platform

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:38.330
for your scalability,
and scaling

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:40.680
to much bigger
capacity, and making it

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:42.350
much easier for your workloads.

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:44.720
And then, last stage is
running Cloud native.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:46.520
That's when you
build your apps--

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:48.824
build your workloads
directly in the cloud.

00:02:48.824 --> 00:02:50.240
And typically what
we see there is

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:53.210
people who will be leveraging
our BigQuery, our Machine

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:56.090
Learning, our Spanner,
App Engine, and of course,

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:58.820
leveraging Cloud Storage
for storing and serving--

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:01.799
analyzing the crucial content.

00:03:01.799 --> 00:03:04.090
Let's talk a little bit about
the evolution of storage.

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:08.270
So we did not arrive in Object
Storage out of the blue.

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:10.780
So it all started with
Direct Attached Storage,

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:13.090
and Direct Attached Storage
is our typical laptops

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:14.490
and desktops.

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:17.017
That's where you have storage
right next to your computer,

00:03:17.017 --> 00:03:18.100
right next to your course.

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:19.730
And everything was fine.

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:20.860
It was growing fine.

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:23.379
People were running
applications on the single box.

00:03:23.379 --> 00:03:25.420
But then people realized
that it's not efficient.

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:28.210
You cannot optimize storage,
and compute at the same time,

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:31.390
and fully utilize your CPUs,
and fully utilize your storage.

00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:34.030
So over time, people realized
that we should separate storage

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:37.000
from compute, and that allows
you to optimize storage

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.940
separately from your compute and
run a full utilization of both

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:40.640
of those.

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:43.150
And that's where SAN came from.

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:45.392
And with SAN, people had to
manage different volumes.

00:03:45.392 --> 00:03:46.850
This is a volume
for this workload.

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:48.220
That's a volume
for that workload.

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:49.886
And they got tired,
and they figured out

00:03:49.886 --> 00:03:52.720
they can move to a level higher
and move to Network Attached

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:54.930
Storage, where they will
break on files instead

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:57.580
of on volumes, and
then over time,

00:03:57.580 --> 00:03:59.120
amount of data just
kept on growing.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:00.940
Now we have huge
amounts of data,

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:03.760
and the industries realize
that they can no longer rely

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:06.280
on file API for
scalability, so they

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:08.100
shifted to very
simple object storage

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:11.230
API that allows you to scale
for huge amounts of data,

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:13.240
petabytes, even
exabytes, and trillions

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:15.469
of objects in the cloud.

00:04:15.469 --> 00:04:17.260
And so today's presentation
will be focused

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:18.259
on Google Cloud Storage.

00:04:18.259 --> 00:04:20.829
That's our object
storage in the cloud.

00:04:20.829 --> 00:04:23.870
And to introduce
Google Cloud Storage,

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:26.530
it's a highly available,
durable, simple, and secure

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:28.870
object storage Google
Cloud platform.

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:31.060
We call it GCS for short.

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:33.790
And we see a lot of success
across different use cases

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:36.176
with GCS and Google
Cloud platform.

00:04:36.176 --> 00:04:38.560
GCS is built on the
same infrastructure

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:40.466
as other internal
services of Google,

00:04:40.466 --> 00:04:43.390
and we actually have quite a
few internal customers at Google

00:04:43.390 --> 00:04:46.840
using underlying infrastructure
for object storage and serving,

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:49.610
and I'm sure you're using lots
of external Google services

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:54.280
that depend on that
exact infrastructure.

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:57.890
I can summarize use cases with
GCS in three large categories.

00:04:57.890 --> 00:05:00.770
So first category is content
storage and delivery.

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:02.270
We see a lot of demand there.

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:05.170
So that's media serving,
image storage, image serving,

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:06.010
music serving.

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:08.200
And some customers
you've heard about

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:09.970
is probably Vimeo
for serving videos,

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:13.300
Spotify for serving music,
and lots of other customers

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:16.630
that store and serve
content out of GCS.

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:18.340
And then moving to
a second category,

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:22.330
that's typically big data
analytics and general compute.

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:24.490
If you have a lot
of data-- petabytes,

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:25.900
hundreds of
petabytes, exabytes--

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:27.880
you've got to store a
huge amount of data.

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:30.180
And for general compute, and
general big data analytics

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:33.370
use cases, you'll probably end
up storing that data in GCS.

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:35.690
And some examples from
different verticals that we see

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:38.530
is health care, different
genomics analytics,

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:41.290
manufacturing, financial
data analytics, e-commerce,

00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:42.850
et cetera, et cetera.

00:05:42.850 --> 00:05:46.380
And last but not least is
backup and archival use cases.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:48.430
And I'm showing quite a
few of our partners here,

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:50.380
and we have a long
list of partners,

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:52.360
that can help you
move to the cloud

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:56.300
and back up or archive
your data to the cloud.

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:58.270
So with GCS, with
Google Cloud Storage,

00:05:58.270 --> 00:06:00.700
we have four use
cases that target

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:02.659
different needs
or your workloads,

00:06:02.659 --> 00:06:04.450
and different needs of
different verticals.

00:06:04.450 --> 00:06:05.740
So I'll start left to right.

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:08.332
So left is our
Multi-Regional storage.

00:06:08.332 --> 00:06:09.790
That's geo-redundant
storage that's

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:11.460
optimized for
business continuity

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:13.580
because it's geo-redundant
out of the box.

00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:16.540
And it's also great for
content serving and delivery

00:06:16.540 --> 00:06:18.910
because data is
distributed on our network.

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:21.752
And it's the highest level
of availability across all

00:06:21.752 --> 00:06:22.710
of the storage classes.

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:24.640
So typical use cases--
it's serving videos,

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:26.650
serving images, and
different websites,

00:06:26.650 --> 00:06:28.640
and different documents as well.

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:30.620
Then, second storage
class is Regional.

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:33.860
Again, it's optimized for
hub storage, hub data,

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:36.105
but it's limited
to a single region.

00:06:36.105 --> 00:06:37.480
And what that
allows you to do is

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:41.556
to have your compute and
storage in one single region

00:06:41.556 --> 00:06:43.180
that we have lowest
latency and highest

00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:45.250
throughput between your
compute and your storage,

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:47.935
and so that's typically
where you see examples

00:06:47.935 --> 00:06:50.290
of video transcoding,
different genomics,

00:06:50.290 --> 00:06:52.054
and different general
computing analytics.

00:06:52.054 --> 00:06:53.470
If you're running
Hadoop workload,

00:06:53.470 --> 00:06:57.670
you'll be using Regional storage
along with your VCE cluster.

00:06:57.670 --> 00:07:00.100
And then going to the right,
we have colder storage.

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:03.280
So Nearline is our storage for
infrequently accessed data.

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:05.347
Rule of thumb which
we typically give

00:07:05.347 --> 00:07:07.930
is if your data is accessed less
than once a month on average,

00:07:07.930 --> 00:07:10.810
or you read 100% of your
data per month or less,

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:13.150
the Nearline will be the
cheaper storage class for you.

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:16.120
And moving to an even colder
tier, that's Coldline.

00:07:16.120 --> 00:07:18.290
With Coldline, it's
still online storage.

00:07:18.290 --> 00:07:21.030
But with Coldline, if you data
access less than once a year

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:24.130
on average, that becomes
your cheapest storage class.

00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:26.560
And typical use cases
we see from customers,

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:29.350
they start with their content
in Multi-Regional or Regional

00:07:29.350 --> 00:07:30.160
storage.

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:32.400
That content is
very actively used.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:34.184
After a couple of
months, people realize

00:07:34.184 --> 00:07:36.350
that that content is no
longer that frequently used,

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:38.640
and they typically tier
it down to Nearline.

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:40.957
So think of your document--
you create a document,

00:07:40.957 --> 00:07:42.790
you will be sharing it
with your colleagues.

00:07:42.790 --> 00:07:44.620
And after a couple of
months, the document

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:46.036
won't be that
frequently accessed,

00:07:46.036 --> 00:07:48.270
so it's natural to tier
it down to Nearline.

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:50.050
And then after a
year, probably no one

00:07:50.050 --> 00:07:51.730
will ever look at
the document again,

00:07:51.730 --> 00:07:54.730
so you can tier down all
the way the Coldline.

00:07:54.730 --> 00:07:58.180
But before you take full
advantage of your storage

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:00.780
classes, you need to
get your data into GCS.

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:02.232
CHRIS TALBOTT: Yeah.

00:08:02.232 --> 00:08:03.440
And we sort of skipped ahead.

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:07.360
So you know, one of
the things that we

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:09.190
talk about here
at Google are some

00:08:09.190 --> 00:08:11.170
of the challenges
associated with how do you

00:08:11.170 --> 00:08:12.670
get your data to the cloud.

00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:16.300
And try as we might, we
can't move stuff faster

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:17.930
than the speed of light.

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:20.892
So depending on the kind
of data that you have,

00:08:20.892 --> 00:08:23.350
the amount of data that you
have for a particular workload,

00:08:23.350 --> 00:08:26.290
and then whatever your
network situation may be,

00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:29.182
you're facing a
wide gamut of ranges

00:08:29.182 --> 00:08:30.640
when it comes to
the amount of time

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:34.520
that it will take to move
that data to the cloud.

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:36.346
In addition to those
sort of challenges

00:08:36.346 --> 00:08:37.929
that come from the
infrastructure side

00:08:37.929 --> 00:08:39.850
and what your situation
may be, you also

00:08:39.850 --> 00:08:42.786
have particular
needs for your role.

00:08:42.786 --> 00:08:44.410
So generally when we
talk to customers,

00:08:44.410 --> 00:08:49.150
we kind of see folks
falling into three buckets--

00:08:49.150 --> 00:08:52.030
IT infrastructure folks,
analysts and marketers,

00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:54.400
and then application developers.

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:57.760
So the IT infrastructure
folks, when we talk to them,

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:00.130
they typically have a
need to move their data

00:09:00.130 --> 00:09:02.740
from either a current
on-premises data center

00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:05.110
or from one cloud to another.

00:09:05.110 --> 00:09:08.710
And what ends up being the
characteristics of their data

00:09:08.710 --> 00:09:10.924
is that it's large
amounts of data.

00:09:10.924 --> 00:09:12.340
It's typically
multiple petabytes.

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:15.220
And they need to bulk upload
that data to the cloud

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:17.860
without affecting their
current network situation

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:20.260
and their current operations.

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:22.240
Now, analysts and
marketers, they're

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:23.797
dealing with data
that's streaming.

00:09:23.797 --> 00:09:25.630
They want real time
insights into that data.

00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:28.000
They want to learn
about their customers

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:29.440
as quickly as
possible by getting

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:32.530
data about their customers
into analytics tools.

00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:34.520
So typically they're
streaming data,

00:09:34.520 --> 00:09:36.480
and they need to
automate data transfer

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:39.280
from particular applications
into analytics tools

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:39.970
in the cloud.

00:09:39.970 --> 00:09:41.630
And then, finally,
developers, they

00:09:41.630 --> 00:09:42.970
need to build applications.

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:48.220
So they want to transfer
their data essentially

00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:51.139
with a natural fit into
their development workflow.

00:09:51.139 --> 00:09:52.930
So they want to use
the network to transfer

00:09:52.930 --> 00:09:55.780
their data into the cloud.

00:09:55.780 --> 00:09:57.400
So whatever your
situation may be,

00:09:57.400 --> 00:09:59.800
whether you're on the
IT infrastructure side,

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:01.480
or you're an analyst
and marketer,

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:05.080
or you're a developer,
we've got options at Google

00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:07.520
for moving that
data into the cloud.

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:11.140
So if you have on-premises
data and a really good network,

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:14.380
you can use our GS util,
our command line interface,

00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:17.800
to move your data
using the network.

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:22.180
If you are in another
cloud storage provider,

00:10:22.180 --> 00:10:26.200
for example, Amazon S3, you can
use a cloud storage transfer

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:29.960
service to move your data
from one cloud to another.

00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:32.620
And if you're on-premises
data, and maybe you

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:35.140
have a bad network situation,
or you have tons of data

00:10:35.140 --> 00:10:37.344
that you need to move,
we have offline option,

00:10:37.344 --> 00:10:39.760
called Transfer Appliance, and
I'll talk a little bit more

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:40.906
about that in a second.

00:10:40.906 --> 00:10:43.030
And then, finally, for
those analysts and marketers

