00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:46.200
So first one is
creating a bucket.

00:12:46.200 --> 00:12:50.190
So there's GS util make bucket,
multi-regional location in US,

00:12:50.190 --> 00:12:52.017
and then name of my
bucket, and that's it.

00:12:52.017 --> 00:12:54.600
As you see, there is no primary,
there is no secondary region.

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:57.390
I'm just specifying that
store my data in the US,

00:12:57.390 --> 00:12:59.400
and then we take care
of all your application

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:00.660
on Google site.

00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:04.290
And then similarly,
retrieving the data with GETs,

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:05.350
standard HTTP GET.

00:13:05.350 --> 00:13:08.550
This is my bucket name,
kirill_us_mr, standard domain

00:13:08.550 --> 00:13:13.170
name from GCS, and then name of
my object, which is image.jpg.

00:13:13.170 --> 00:13:16.641
And again, there is no primary,
there is no secondary location.

00:13:16.641 --> 00:13:18.390
What's cool about it,
if something happens

00:13:18.390 --> 00:13:19.830
to one of our regions,
we'll automatically

00:13:19.830 --> 00:13:22.410
fail over and route your
request to a secondary location

00:13:22.410 --> 00:13:25.240
or tertiary location,
wherever we have your data.

00:13:25.240 --> 00:13:27.870
And that means no
downtime in case

00:13:27.870 --> 00:13:29.362
of regional failure
on your side,

00:13:29.362 --> 00:13:30.945
because you do need
to manually switch

00:13:30.945 --> 00:13:32.540
from primary to
secondary, or worry

00:13:32.540 --> 00:13:34.680
about implementing
that code, or switching

00:13:34.680 --> 00:13:37.170
from primary to secondary.

00:13:37.170 --> 00:13:38.640
In a similar way,
the storage class

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:40.770
is optimized for
end user latency.

00:13:40.770 --> 00:13:43.770
So we'll be serving data from
the closest available region

00:13:43.770 --> 00:13:45.720
or closest available
location from where

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:46.890
the request came from.

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:49.152
We'll also cache
data in the regions

00:13:49.152 --> 00:13:50.610
that are nearest
to those end users

00:13:50.610 --> 00:13:52.410
that are accessing the data.

00:13:52.410 --> 00:13:54.820
And if you mark
the data as public,

00:13:54.820 --> 00:13:56.190
that means it's cacheable.

00:13:56.190 --> 00:13:57.960
And far edges of
our network, we'll

00:13:57.960 --> 00:14:00.810
cache it in more than
100 points of presence

00:14:00.810 --> 00:14:03.440
from where the data
was accessed from.

00:14:03.440 --> 00:14:05.570
And to talk a little bit
more about the caching,

00:14:05.570 --> 00:14:07.070
there is quite a
few things that are

00:14:07.070 --> 00:14:09.486
embedded to this Multi-Regional
storage in GCS in general.

00:14:09.486 --> 00:14:11.720
So I want to spend
a minute and explain

00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:13.820
how does this Multi-Regional
storage compare

00:14:13.820 --> 00:14:15.860
to standard solutions
in the market.

00:14:15.860 --> 00:14:17.600
Typically what you
see from other cloud

00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:19.610
providers or
standard applications

00:14:19.610 --> 00:14:22.190
how you store your
data, your consumers

00:14:22.190 --> 00:14:25.580
would go directly over public
internet or different providers

00:14:25.580 --> 00:14:27.830
to a location, or that region
of the data they stored.

00:14:27.830 --> 00:14:30.080
For instance, in this picture,
consumers from a laptop

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:31.880
was going directly to Region B.

00:14:31.880 --> 00:14:34.416
With GCS, our approach
is very different.

00:14:34.416 --> 00:14:37.040
So you'll have the closest point
of presence in Google network.

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:39.380
And Google network is huge.

00:14:39.380 --> 00:14:41.870
Google works really well, and
we leverage the same network

00:14:41.870 --> 00:14:45.290
with GCS, and still we have
the closest point of presence.

00:14:45.290 --> 00:14:47.210
If your objects are
marked as public,

00:14:47.210 --> 00:14:49.760
we'll check in the cache, do
we have this data in the cache?

00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:51.801
And then we'll serve it
directly out of the cache

00:14:51.801 --> 00:14:54.000
without going all the
way to storage level.

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:56.210
And if we don't have
the data in the cache,

00:14:56.210 --> 00:14:59.120
we'll figure out which region is
closest to this location-- it's

00:14:59.120 --> 00:15:01.010
Region A or Region B,
and we'll serve data

00:15:01.010 --> 00:15:04.490
directly out of that region,
whichever one is closest.

00:15:04.490 --> 00:15:07.040
And that really helps
optimize end user latency.

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:09.497
And some of our customers,
for highly cacheable data,

00:15:09.497 --> 00:15:11.080
they get insane
performance, they get,

00:15:11.080 --> 00:15:13.880
like, millisecond-level
performance of serving data out

00:15:13.880 --> 00:15:17.540
of those ops.

00:15:17.540 --> 00:15:20.610
In terms of pricing, as I said
before, pricing is very simple.

00:15:20.610 --> 00:15:23.947
We charge a simple
$0.026 per gigabyte month

00:15:23.947 --> 00:15:25.280
in this Multi-Regional location.

00:15:25.280 --> 00:15:26.600
And that's for logical data.

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:29.141
We don't charge you for primary
replica or secondary replica.

00:15:29.141 --> 00:15:30.980
We don't charge you
for replication of data

00:15:30.980 --> 00:15:32.450
between those two regions.

00:15:32.450 --> 00:15:35.430
Everything is included, in
terms of storage, in this price.

00:15:35.430 --> 00:15:38.272
And so it's geo-redundant, we
take care of this replication.

00:15:38.272 --> 00:15:40.480
For reads and writes, we
have industry standard price

00:15:40.480 --> 00:15:41.540
for how much we charge.

00:15:41.540 --> 00:15:44.160
And it's really
super low, $0.004

00:15:44.160 --> 00:15:47.130
per 10,000 operations
for reads, and $0.005

00:15:47.130 --> 00:15:48.170
per 1,000 operations.

00:15:48.170 --> 00:15:50.405
Writes, we provide highest
level of availability.

00:15:50.405 --> 00:15:53.030
That's 3 and 1/2
nines availability SLA

00:15:53.030 --> 00:15:54.080
for this product.

00:15:54.080 --> 00:15:55.547
And it operates
even higher-- it's

00:15:55.547 --> 00:15:57.630
designed for even higher
availability than these 3

00:15:57.630 --> 00:15:59.120
and 1/2 nines.

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:02.170
And again, industry standard
11 nines or annual durability.

00:16:02.170 --> 00:16:04.160
And we have the same
11 nines across all

00:16:04.160 --> 00:16:07.820
of our products for durability.

00:16:07.820 --> 00:16:10.466
Before we deep dive into how
to optimize your performance

00:16:10.466 --> 00:16:11.840
with different
storage classes, I

00:16:11.840 --> 00:16:14.690
want to show you how we lay
out data in simple terms.

00:16:14.690 --> 00:16:17.240
And that really will
help you intuition,

00:16:17.240 --> 00:16:19.910
or how to use the storage
classes in which situations.

00:16:19.910 --> 00:16:22.850
So starting with multi-regional,
very simplistic view.

00:16:22.850 --> 00:16:25.610
It's two replicas, and we
have these two replicas

00:16:25.610 --> 00:16:26.910
included for regions.

00:16:26.910 --> 00:16:29.960
So as you see in this chart, you
may not have all of your data

00:16:29.960 --> 00:16:31.050
in each particular region.

00:16:31.050 --> 00:16:32.591
So you will not have
all of your data

00:16:32.591 --> 00:16:35.390
in Region A or Region B or
Region C. But for each object,

00:16:35.390 --> 00:16:37.880
we'll have two replicas
that are geo-redundant,

00:16:37.880 --> 00:16:39.795
and with the geo-redundant--
that replication

00:16:39.795 --> 00:16:41.570
in a synchronous way.

00:16:41.570 --> 00:16:44.060
And so for instance, if
you're serving this content,

00:16:44.060 --> 00:16:45.560
there is no guarantee
that all of it

00:16:45.560 --> 00:16:47.340
will be in each
particular region.

00:16:47.340 --> 00:16:49.960
But we'll have the
replicas of that content.

00:16:49.960 --> 00:16:52.479
Then going to Regional storage,
the situation with Regional

00:16:52.479 --> 00:16:53.020
is different.

00:16:53.020 --> 00:16:55.700
So for instance, if you selected
Region B for your Regional

00:16:55.700 --> 00:16:57.590
storage, all of
your data will be

00:16:57.590 --> 00:17:00.410
in that Region B. It will be
redundant across availability

00:17:00.410 --> 00:17:02.653
zones within Region
B, and all of it

00:17:02.653 --> 00:17:04.069
will be within
that single region.

00:17:04.069 --> 00:17:06.890
So that means if you
select your compute cluster

00:17:06.890 --> 00:17:09.526
in that same region, you'll have
highest level of performance

00:17:09.526 --> 00:17:11.900
between your storage and
compute because all of your data

00:17:11.900 --> 00:17:12.859
is in that region.

00:17:12.859 --> 00:17:15.530
You don't need to go across
regions on our network.

00:17:15.530 --> 00:17:17.300
And then with
Nearline and Coldline,

00:17:17.300 --> 00:17:20.119
if you select Multi-Regional
location for Nearline

00:17:20.119 --> 00:17:21.920
and Coldline, those
two storage classes

00:17:21.920 --> 00:17:24.710
are not geo-redundant,
which is different from our

00:17:24.710 --> 00:17:26.000
Multi-Regional storage class.

00:17:26.000 --> 00:17:27.200
But with Nearline
and Coldline, it

00:17:27.200 --> 00:17:28.980
will be stored in one
of the regions that

00:17:28.980 --> 00:17:30.650
are parked with multi-region.

00:17:30.650 --> 00:17:32.150
With Nearline and
Coldline, you also

00:17:32.150 --> 00:17:34.108
have an option to select
the regional location.

00:17:34.108 --> 00:17:36.720
You can say, store all my data
in Region B, and in that case,

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:39.053
it will behave very similarly
to Regional storage class,

00:17:39.053 --> 00:17:42.530
where all of the data
is in one region.

00:17:42.530 --> 00:17:44.730
Let's talk a little bit
about those best practices.

00:17:44.730 --> 00:17:47.390
So as you might
understand, you want

00:17:47.390 --> 00:17:49.190
to take full advantage
of our network.

00:17:49.190 --> 00:17:50.960
And when you think
about our network,

00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:52.607
you don't want to
proxy everything

00:17:52.607 --> 00:17:53.690
through a single location.

00:17:53.690 --> 00:17:55.280
You want your end
consumers directly

00:17:55.280 --> 00:17:57.200
accessing GCS, and
that will allow

00:17:57.200 --> 00:18:01.595
you to scale to Gbps 1
terabit per second speeds,

00:18:01.595 --> 00:18:03.970
because you'll be taking full
advantage of Google network

00:18:03.970 --> 00:18:06.950
instead of trying to go
through a single endpoint,

00:18:06.950 --> 00:18:08.720
or single hub.

00:18:08.720 --> 00:18:11.000
And that's really the
way for our customers

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:13.990
to scale to huge, huge
volumes of traffic.

00:18:13.990 --> 00:18:16.490
Then, similarly,
if you have lots

00:18:16.490 --> 00:18:18.390
of requests coming
to those objects,

00:18:18.390 --> 00:18:20.480
please parallelize them,
because that will also

00:18:20.480 --> 00:18:22.480
allow you to take full
advantage of our network,

00:18:22.480 --> 00:18:24.380
with having lots of
parallel requests

00:18:24.380 --> 00:18:26.060
coming in at the same time.

00:18:26.060 --> 00:18:29.450
And then favor larger requests,
because larger objects

00:18:29.450 --> 00:18:31.765
will move faster on
our amazing network.

00:18:31.765 --> 00:18:33.140
And parallelize
small operations.

00:18:33.140 --> 00:18:34.790
Just keep parallelizing
everything.

00:18:34.790 --> 00:18:36.740
Don't have those
sequential operations.

00:18:36.740 --> 00:18:38.198
Like, it doesn't
make sense to have

00:18:38.198 --> 00:18:40.070
millions of
sequential operations

00:18:40.070 --> 00:18:41.990
if you can parallelize them.

00:18:41.990 --> 00:18:44.780
All of them will complete faster
if they're going in parallel.

00:18:44.780 --> 00:18:45.950
And some rules of thumb--

00:18:45.950 --> 00:18:49.720
for a single stream of write,
we have roughly half a Gbps.

00:18:49.720 --> 00:18:52.520
For a single stream of read,
we have roughly 1 Gbps.

00:18:52.520 --> 00:18:54.980
And if you parallelize,
it scales almost linearly,

00:18:54.980 --> 00:18:59.312
with having multiple streams
accessing GCS in parallel.

