00:23:23.595 --> 00:23:25.250
KIRILL TROPIN: Large chicken.

00:23:25.250 --> 00:23:28.150
All right, so let's switch
back to our storage classes.

00:23:28.150 --> 00:23:30.150
And we'll talk about
Regional storage class now.

00:23:30.150 --> 00:23:32.332
So Regional storage
is the storage

00:23:32.332 --> 00:23:34.290
that's best for data
analytics, and it's really

00:23:34.290 --> 00:23:36.950
best for data analytics, because
you can select a region where

00:23:36.950 --> 00:23:38.991
to store your data, and
you can run your compute,

00:23:38.991 --> 00:23:42.569
and run different Cloud Platform
tools within the same region.

00:23:42.569 --> 00:23:43.860
It's redundant within a region.

00:23:43.860 --> 00:23:45.901
It's redundant across
multiple availability zones

00:23:45.901 --> 00:23:48.280
to achieve that high
level of availability.

00:23:48.280 --> 00:23:51.020
And it's integrated with all
the standard Cloud Platform

00:23:51.020 --> 00:23:55.680
tools or products, like
Data Floor, ML, BigQuery,

00:23:55.680 --> 00:23:58.620
Cloud Genomics, and
obviously, Compute Engine.

00:23:58.620 --> 00:24:01.080
But this is really
the storage class

00:24:01.080 --> 00:24:03.100
to use with all those tools.

00:24:03.100 --> 00:24:06.390
It's ideal for running general
compute within a single region.

00:24:06.390 --> 00:24:08.400
Different data analytics
use cases with Hadoop,

00:24:08.400 --> 00:24:11.070
with Cloud DataProc,
data flow, also

00:24:11.070 --> 00:24:13.560
for serving that content
within one constrained

00:24:13.560 --> 00:24:14.977
geographic location.

00:24:14.977 --> 00:24:16.560
And you can also use
the storage class

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:19.260
for region shielding with
content delivery networks

00:24:19.260 --> 00:24:21.930
if you want to have
predictability and latency

00:24:21.930 --> 00:24:23.910
where your data is
stored, and where

00:24:23.910 --> 00:24:28.960
you're exiting our network with
CDN shielding, for instance.

00:24:28.960 --> 00:24:31.250
Let me show you an example
of using this storage

00:24:31.250 --> 00:24:32.240
class with Hadoop.

00:24:32.240 --> 00:24:33.990
So we talked about
that lift and shift use

00:24:33.990 --> 00:24:36.540
case for our customers in
their journey to cloud.

00:24:36.540 --> 00:24:39.040
And for customers that already
have Hadoop clusters on-prem,

00:24:39.040 --> 00:24:41.081
and they want to lift and
shift them to the cloud

00:24:41.081 --> 00:24:42.660
and run that workload
in the cloud,

00:24:42.660 --> 00:24:44.118
we have a perfect
product for that.

00:24:44.118 --> 00:24:45.530
It's called Cloud DataProc.

00:24:45.530 --> 00:24:47.670
It's essentially a
managed Spark and Hadoop

00:24:47.670 --> 00:24:49.530
service on Cloud Storage.

00:24:49.530 --> 00:24:51.050
And what's cool
about DataProc is

00:24:51.050 --> 00:24:52.550
that it has Cloud
Storage connector,

00:24:52.550 --> 00:24:54.750
and that connector
is HDFS-compliant.

00:24:54.750 --> 00:24:58.775
So that means to migrate to
your storage GCS with Hadoop,

00:24:58.775 --> 00:25:00.150
all you need to
do is just change

00:25:00.150 --> 00:25:02.250
your pointer to
where data is stored.

00:25:02.250 --> 00:25:06.930
Instead of having HCFS pointer,
just having a GS pointer.

00:25:06.930 --> 00:25:07.731
And that's it.

00:25:07.731 --> 00:25:09.980
You don't need to do any
other change to your storage.

00:25:09.980 --> 00:25:11.760
You just change
where data is stored,

00:25:11.760 --> 00:25:15.060
and essentially upload it
to GCS, change from HCFS

00:25:15.060 --> 00:25:17.340
the to GS, and you're done.

00:25:17.340 --> 00:25:18.900
Everything else will
keep on working.

00:25:18.900 --> 00:25:21.350
And what's great about
using Google Cloud

00:25:21.350 --> 00:25:23.310
Storage versus
using Hadoop on-prem

00:25:23.310 --> 00:25:25.220
is that you can shut
down your cluster,

00:25:25.220 --> 00:25:27.030
and your data will
still be there in GCS.

00:25:27.030 --> 00:25:29.250
You can spin off another
cluster and access the data.

00:25:29.250 --> 00:25:33.141
So the data is accessible when
your cluster is turned on,

00:25:33.141 --> 00:25:34.890
and that allows you
to save a lot of money

00:25:34.890 --> 00:25:36.030
on that compute capacity.

00:25:36.030 --> 00:25:38.430
You can scale down, scale
up your computer cluster

00:25:38.430 --> 00:25:40.080
independently from storage.

00:25:40.080 --> 00:25:42.660
And you can learn more about
DataProc and that Cloud Storage

00:25:42.660 --> 00:25:47.407
connector on
Cloud.google.com/DataProc.

00:25:47.407 --> 00:25:48.990
In terms of pricing,
pricing structure

00:25:48.990 --> 00:25:50.530
is very similar
to multi-regional.

00:25:50.530 --> 00:25:51.810
Again, it's simple.

00:25:51.810 --> 00:25:54.910
In case of regional, it's
$0.02 per gigabyte month

00:25:54.910 --> 00:25:57.175
versus $0.026 per
gigabyte month.

00:25:57.175 --> 00:25:59.300
And pricing is slightly
different around the world,

00:25:59.300 --> 00:26:01.750
but this is our typical
price in US, Europe,

00:26:01.750 --> 00:26:04.366
and the major locations in Asia.

00:26:04.366 --> 00:26:05.740
Read operations,
write operations

00:26:05.740 --> 00:26:08.090
are the same as with
Multi-Regional storage.

00:26:08.090 --> 00:26:09.880
Our availability
SLA is three nines,

00:26:09.880 --> 00:26:13.644
and this product is designed
for even higher availability.

00:26:13.644 --> 00:26:15.810
Again, all the standard
stuff-- the same durability,

00:26:15.810 --> 00:26:17.599
the same instant
access, the same API.

00:26:17.599 --> 00:26:19.390
So as you saw, it was
very simple to create

00:26:19.390 --> 00:26:21.040
Multi-Regional bucket.

00:26:21.040 --> 00:26:22.950
API is exactly the same
for Regional storage.

00:26:22.950 --> 00:26:24.491
You just specify
that's it's Regional

00:26:24.491 --> 00:26:27.010
storage and not Multi-Regional.

00:26:27.010 --> 00:26:29.750
In terms of best practices for
using Regional and optimizing

00:26:29.750 --> 00:26:31.810
that performance,
when you are running

00:26:31.810 --> 00:26:34.540
those analytics workloads,
when you're accessing storage

00:26:34.540 --> 00:26:37.120
from Compute Engine, you
should be co-locating your data

00:26:37.120 --> 00:26:37.630
in compute.

00:26:37.630 --> 00:26:40.015
You should be really selecting
the same region for storing

00:26:40.015 --> 00:26:41.800
your data and where you
are on your compute jobs,

00:26:41.800 --> 00:26:43.420
because it decreases
your latency,

00:26:43.420 --> 00:26:45.840
makes sure you have
the highest throughput.

00:26:45.840 --> 00:26:47.800
It also optimizes
your predictability,

00:26:47.800 --> 00:26:50.350
because you have predictable
tail latency since your data is

00:26:50.350 --> 00:26:51.880
collocated with your compute.

00:26:51.880 --> 00:26:54.760
And all the standard stuff--
parallelize your operations,

00:26:54.760 --> 00:26:57.250
because you can still take
full advantage of the network

00:26:57.250 --> 00:27:00.020
in a single region.

00:27:00.020 --> 00:27:01.204
Let's talk about Cool Data.

00:27:01.204 --> 00:27:03.120
So for Cool Data, we
have two storage classes,

00:27:03.120 --> 00:27:04.580
that's Nearline and Coldline.

00:27:04.580 --> 00:27:05.950
I'll start with Nearline.

00:27:05.950 --> 00:27:07.640
So Nearline is our
storage class that's

00:27:07.640 --> 00:27:09.865
best for infrequently
accessed data.

00:27:09.865 --> 00:27:13.430
It has the same online latency,
the same API as other storage

00:27:13.430 --> 00:27:14.610
classes.

00:27:14.610 --> 00:27:17.570
Common use case is
serving long-tail content.

00:27:17.570 --> 00:27:19.360
So remember, I said
it has online latency.

00:27:19.360 --> 00:27:20.985
So you can still use
this storage class

00:27:20.985 --> 00:27:22.880
for serving your content.

00:27:22.880 --> 00:27:26.669
But if that content is
not that actively used,

00:27:26.669 --> 00:27:28.710
just put it in Nearline
and serve it directly out

00:27:28.710 --> 00:27:29.990
of Nearline.

00:27:29.990 --> 00:27:32.300
In terms of storage price,
we charge significantly less

00:27:32.300 --> 00:27:34.640
We charge only $0.01
per gigabyte month,

00:27:34.640 --> 00:27:37.280
but we charge $0.01
per gigabyte read.

00:27:37.280 --> 00:27:39.489
And those two numbers work
out to that rule of thumb,

00:27:39.489 --> 00:27:41.654
that if your data is accessed
less than once a month

00:27:41.654 --> 00:27:43.930
on average, Nearline is
really the cheapest storage

00:27:43.930 --> 00:27:45.360
class for you.

00:27:45.360 --> 00:27:47.660
A couple other differences
versus Nearline

00:27:47.660 --> 00:27:50.540
and Regional-- so minimal
storage duration in Nearline

00:27:50.540 --> 00:27:51.260
is 30 days.

00:27:51.260 --> 00:27:53.860
We did not have that concept
for our Hot Storage class.

00:27:53.860 --> 00:27:56.810
With Nearline, we have it simply
because we charge so little

00:27:56.810 --> 00:27:58.130
for that storage.

00:27:58.130 --> 00:27:59.560
And this storage
class is designed

00:27:59.560 --> 00:28:02.330
for longer-lived content,
versus Regional, where you

00:28:02.330 --> 00:28:04.160
can have temporary stored data.

00:28:04.160 --> 00:28:06.740
And then Slightly Lower
Availability, SLA, again, we

00:28:06.740 --> 00:28:09.690
optimized this product
for that low price--

00:28:09.690 --> 00:28:11.760
lower price-- and
infrequent access.

00:28:11.760 --> 00:28:14.150
So our availability
SLA is two nines,

00:28:14.150 --> 00:28:17.466
but it's designed to run at
three nines of availability.

00:28:17.466 --> 00:28:19.140
And then moving to Coldline.

00:28:19.140 --> 00:28:22.290
So Coldline, you'll see very,
very similar to Nearline.

00:28:22.290 --> 00:28:24.870
So with Coldline, we
still have online latency.

00:28:24.870 --> 00:28:26.745
You can still actually
use the storage class

00:28:26.745 --> 00:28:28.340
for serving your content.

00:28:28.340 --> 00:28:30.895
It's the same consistent
API as any other--

00:28:30.895 --> 00:28:32.520
CHRIS TALBOTT: Wait
one second, though.

00:28:32.520 --> 00:28:35.647
So it's archived storage
that has millisecond latency?

00:28:35.647 --> 00:28:36.480
KIRILL TROPIN: Yeah.

00:28:36.480 --> 00:28:38.970
It's archived storage
with online latency.

00:28:38.970 --> 00:28:41.610
As I say, it's archived storage
that you can actually use.

00:28:41.610 --> 00:28:43.950
Because you can archive data,
and whenever you need it,

00:28:43.950 --> 00:28:46.835
you can still access it,
use it any time you need.

00:28:46.835 --> 00:28:48.960
You don't need to wait for
that retrieval from tape

00:28:48.960 --> 00:28:52.080
or any other semi-offline
or offline storage

00:28:52.080 --> 00:28:53.100
that you're using.

00:28:53.100 --> 00:28:55.020
And we have some customers
that actually use

00:28:55.020 --> 00:28:56.670
Coldline for serving content.

00:28:56.670 --> 00:28:59.270
Customers that have just huge
amount of content, that really

00:28:59.270 --> 00:29:01.990
long-tail content, they put
some parts of the content

00:29:01.990 --> 00:29:03.930
in Coldline, and they
still serve it out

00:29:03.930 --> 00:29:06.660
just because it's not
accessed frequently.

00:29:06.660 --> 00:29:08.850
And in terms of
pricing, it's lower

00:29:08.850 --> 00:29:10.110
than Nearline for storage.

00:29:10.110 --> 00:29:12.090
It's $0.007 cents
per gigabyte month.

00:29:12.090 --> 00:29:13.567
And we charge more
for data access,

00:29:13.567 --> 00:29:15.775
and those two numbers work
out to that rule of thumb.

00:29:15.775 --> 00:29:18.450
If your data is accessed less
than once a year on average,

00:29:18.450 --> 00:29:22.430
then Coldline is the
cheapest storage class.

00:29:22.430 --> 00:29:25.510
Just to show you how consistent
that API and that performance

00:29:25.510 --> 00:29:26.090
is--

00:29:26.090 --> 00:29:28.420
so with API it will
look exactly the same

00:29:28.420 --> 00:29:30.350
I was showing with the
Multi-Regional storage.

00:29:30.350 --> 00:29:31.820
So my bucket is called coldline.

00:29:31.820 --> 00:29:34.460
I've claimed that name
for my storage class.

00:29:34.460 --> 00:29:38.390
Again, same domain name and
image.jpg, and standard GET/PUT

00:29:38.390 --> 00:29:40.660
interface for getting
the data in and out.

00:29:40.660 --> 00:29:43.010
For performance, I'm
showing GS util perf diag,

00:29:43.010 --> 00:29:46.280
so it's similar to the tool
that Chris was showing,

00:29:46.280 --> 00:29:48.500
but with perf diag is our
perf diagnostic as part

00:29:48.500 --> 00:29:50.990
of GS util, which is
our storage utility.

00:29:50.990 --> 00:29:53.110
And here I was downloading
1 megabyte objects

00:29:53.110 --> 00:29:54.260
out of Coldline.

00:29:54.260 --> 00:29:58.245
I was not as far as London
versus US in that example,

00:29:58.245 --> 00:30:00.620
but in that case, when I was
testing it, I was in London,

00:30:00.620 --> 00:30:01.760
and data was in Belgium.

00:30:01.760 --> 00:30:03.517
So there was a bit
of extra help there.

00:30:03.517 --> 00:30:05.975
And you can see in this example,
its consistent performance

00:30:05.975 --> 00:30:09.440
is between 147
milliseconds up to 200

00:30:09.440 --> 00:30:11.870
millisecond for uploading
that 1 megabyte object out

00:30:11.870 --> 00:30:12.469
of Coldline.

00:30:12.469 --> 00:30:13.010
That's crazy.

00:30:13.010 --> 00:30:14.020
That's our archived storage.

00:30:14.020 --> 00:30:15.603
CHRIS TALBOTT: So
if we had wanted to,

00:30:15.603 --> 00:30:18.565
I could have served that Lego
chicken image out of Coldline.

00:30:18.565 --> 00:30:20.690
If someone was requesting
that image, for instance,

00:30:20.690 --> 00:30:22.460
and it was giving them
the same experience

00:30:22.460 --> 00:30:24.650
had we had it in
another storage class.

00:30:24.650 --> 00:30:25.190
KIRILL TROPIN: That's right.

00:30:25.190 --> 00:30:26.300
It's still online storage.

00:30:26.300 --> 00:30:28.091
It still relies on the
same Google network,

00:30:28.091 --> 00:30:30.651
so it would be just as fast.

00:30:30.651 --> 00:30:32.650
Let's talk about using
storage classes together.

00:30:32.650 --> 00:30:34.900
I want to remind you again,
all of the storage classes

00:30:34.900 --> 00:30:38.350
that we have, because we have
quite a few of them, four.

00:30:38.350 --> 00:30:41.140
And so Multi-Regional is our
storage for content serving

00:30:41.140 --> 00:30:41.710
and delivery.

00:30:41.710 --> 00:30:42.530
It's geo-redundant.

00:30:42.530 --> 00:30:44.530
It's available in three
multi-regional locations

00:30:44.530 --> 00:30:46.840
worldwide: US, Europe, and Asia.

00:30:46.840 --> 00:30:49.105
And we charged the $0.026
per gigabyte month,

00:30:49.105 --> 00:30:51.220
a simple price per
logical gigabyte

00:30:51.220 --> 00:30:52.350
month if you are storing.

00:30:52.350 --> 00:30:54.220
Then going to the
right, we have Regional,

00:30:54.220 --> 00:30:56.980
which is storage of PMIs for
access within a single region,

00:30:56.980 --> 00:30:59.440
be we charge $0.02 per
gigabyte month of storage.

00:30:59.440 --> 00:31:00.580
So it's slightly cheaper.

00:31:00.580 --> 00:31:02.496
And then Nearline and
Coldline is infrequently

00:31:02.496 --> 00:31:04.670
accessed storage
and cold storage.

00:31:04.670 --> 00:31:07.860
And for those storage classes
we charge much less for storage,

00:31:07.860 --> 00:31:09.730
but we charge more for access.

00:31:09.730 --> 00:31:11.200
And a typical rule of thumb--

00:31:11.200 --> 00:31:14.380
for Nearline, it's data that is
accessed once a month or less;

00:31:14.380 --> 00:31:16.382
and Coldline, accessed
once a year or less.

00:31:16.382 --> 00:31:18.340
And what's cool about
all these storage classes

00:31:18.340 --> 00:31:20.080
is that they have the same API.

00:31:20.080 --> 00:31:23.350
And having that API allowed us
to have object level storage

00:31:23.350 --> 00:31:26.512
class and data lifecycle across
all of those storage classes,

00:31:26.512 --> 00:31:28.720
and you can really transition
between storage classes

00:31:28.720 --> 00:31:31.390
very smoothly, and
without changing URL,

00:31:31.390 --> 00:31:33.834
without changing your API,
how you interact with data.

00:31:33.834 --> 00:31:36.250
You can simply change storage
class, and all of your tools

00:31:36.250 --> 00:31:38.370
will keep working.

00:31:38.370 --> 00:31:40.495
So to simplify that
lifecycle for you,

00:31:40.495 --> 00:31:43.050
we created Data Lifecycle
across storage classes.

00:31:43.050 --> 00:31:45.780
It's a feature that
launched about a year ago,

00:31:45.780 --> 00:31:48.240
and we allow you to
automatically set

00:31:48.240 --> 00:31:50.370
policies and transition
data from hot storage

00:31:50.370 --> 00:31:52.650
to cold storage based
on the age of the data.

00:31:52.650 --> 00:31:55.620
So a typical example that
we see is for documents,

00:31:55.620 --> 00:31:57.300
as I was telling earlier.

00:31:57.300 --> 00:31:59.130
Within the first 30
days, that document

00:31:59.130 --> 00:32:01.700
is accessed very frequently,
people interacting, editing

00:32:01.700 --> 00:32:03.660
the document, and
so you will probably

00:32:03.660 --> 00:32:05.790
be storing it in
Multi-Regional storage class.

00:32:05.790 --> 00:32:07.830
After 30 days, you
can set a policy

00:32:07.830 --> 00:32:10.620
to move it down or tier it
down to Nearline, and then

00:32:10.620 --> 00:32:12.930
after another 90
days, or 180 days, you

00:32:12.930 --> 00:32:15.390
can, say, tier down
to Coldline, and GCS

00:32:15.390 --> 00:32:17.217
will take care of
that tiering down,

00:32:17.217 --> 00:32:18.800
and you don't need
to change anything.

00:32:18.800 --> 00:32:20.883
Your API's the same, your
bucket name is the same,

00:32:20.883 --> 00:32:22.556
your object name was the same.

00:32:22.556 --> 00:32:24.180
All of your code
would keep on working.

00:32:24.180 --> 00:32:27.850
Even if that document ends
up in our coldest tier,

00:32:27.850 --> 00:32:32.130
all the stuff will keep on
working with the same API.

00:32:32.130 --> 00:32:34.950
All right, so some closing
notes before we switch to Q&amp;A.

00:32:34.950 --> 00:32:37.290
So I want to remind, we
have four storage classes.

00:32:37.290 --> 00:32:40.276
They're tuned for different
use cases for object storage.

00:32:40.276 --> 00:32:42.150
We have the same API,
the same online latency

00:32:42.150 --> 00:32:43.339
across all of them.

00:32:43.339 --> 00:32:45.630
And that really simplifies
your management of your data

00:32:45.630 --> 00:32:47.160
and how you access the data.

00:32:47.160 --> 00:32:50.070
All of these storage classes
rely on Google network.

00:32:50.070 --> 00:32:52.250
And our peering, we
have open peering policy

00:32:52.250 --> 00:32:54.010
with Google Cloud Platform.

00:32:54.010 --> 00:32:56.781
We have lifecycle across
all of the storage classes.

00:32:56.781 --> 00:32:59.280
You can learn more about us if
you just Google "Google Cloud

00:32:59.280 --> 00:33:02.430
Storage," or just go to
Cloud.google.com/storage.

00:33:02.430 --> 00:33:06.040
We have free tier and
free trial with GCS,

00:33:06.040 --> 00:33:07.630
so please go ahead
and try it out.

00:33:07.630 --> 00:33:11.260
Upload your Lego chickens
and see how fast they are.

00:33:11.260 --> 00:33:11.760
Yeah.

00:33:11.760 --> 00:33:12.997
That's all I have.

00:33:12.997 --> 00:33:14.580
So we're going to
take a minute break,

00:33:14.580 --> 00:33:16.350
and then we'll switch to
Q&amp;A, and we'll be back.

00:33:16.350 --> 00:33:16.590
CHRIS TALBOTT: Yeah.

00:33:16.590 --> 00:33:17.430
Thank you so much.

00:33:17.430 --> 00:33:18.471
KIRILL TROPIN: Thank you.

00:34:47.530 --> 00:34:48.050
All right.

00:34:48.050 --> 00:34:49.300
Thank you for listening to us.

00:34:49.300 --> 00:34:51.610
We have quite a few
questions coming online.

00:34:51.610 --> 00:34:54.100
And so we'll try to answer
as many of them as we can.

00:34:54.100 --> 00:34:55.780
So we'll start
with the first one,

00:34:55.780 --> 00:34:58.616
when will GCS be
available in my country?

00:34:58.616 --> 00:35:00.490
And so what's interesting
about this question

00:35:00.490 --> 00:35:02.864
is the difference between when
it's going to be available

00:35:02.864 --> 00:35:04.780
versus when it can
store data in GCS.

00:35:04.780 --> 00:35:06.156
So GCS is really
available pretty

00:35:06.156 --> 00:35:08.738
much in all the countries in the
world where you have internet

00:35:08.738 --> 00:35:10.870
with just a few exceptions
where we're not allowed

00:35:10.870 --> 00:35:12.610
to have Google services.

00:35:12.610 --> 00:35:14.880
And as we talked
about, our network,

00:35:14.880 --> 00:35:17.176
we have 100 points of
presence around the world.

00:35:17.176 --> 00:35:18.550
And wherever you
store your data,

00:35:18.550 --> 00:35:19.870
we'll be delivering
it to our network

00:35:19.870 --> 00:35:21.430
to the closest
point of presence,

00:35:21.430 --> 00:35:24.220
and then within public
internet within that country,

00:35:24.220 --> 00:35:26.770
even if we don't have that
point of presence with Google.

00:35:26.770 --> 00:35:29.459
So really, you can store
data anywhere in GCS

00:35:29.459 --> 00:35:31.000
in those locations
that are available

00:35:31.000 --> 00:35:32.541
and access the data
around the world.

00:35:32.541 --> 00:35:35.650
So it's really
available worldwide.

00:35:35.650 --> 00:35:38.350
So second question, can I
use GCS as a Content Delivery

00:35:38.350 --> 00:35:40.000
Network or CDN?

00:35:40.000 --> 00:35:41.312
Yes, you can use it.

00:35:41.312 --> 00:35:43.270
Because like we talked
about that edge-caching,

00:35:43.270 --> 00:35:45.550
it sounds quite familiar
or quite similar to CDN

00:35:45.550 --> 00:35:46.050
technology.

00:35:46.050 --> 00:35:48.258
But surely, if you want to
optimize your performance,

00:35:48.258 --> 00:35:49.750
if you want to
optimize your cost,

00:35:49.750 --> 00:35:53.900
you can use GCS as origin
or storage for the content,

00:35:53.900 --> 00:35:56.352
and then use our
Google Cloud CDN

00:35:56.352 --> 00:35:57.560
and some of our CDN partners.

00:35:57.560 --> 00:35:59.768
If you're already using CDN,
it's pretty easy for you

00:35:59.768 --> 00:36:02.980
to transition, move that
origin storage into GCS

00:36:02.980 --> 00:36:06.020
while keeping your CDN provider
that you're using today.

00:36:06.020 --> 00:36:08.740
And even if you
don't need that CDN,

00:36:08.740 --> 00:36:11.420
even if you don't have that much
public or cacheable content,

00:36:11.420 --> 00:36:14.825
GCS will serve as a basic
CDN with caching that content

00:36:14.825 --> 00:36:16.690
on the edge.

00:36:16.690 --> 00:36:18.670
All right, third
question, can I control

00:36:18.670 --> 00:36:20.350
where my data is stored?

00:36:20.350 --> 00:36:21.220
Absolutely.

00:36:21.220 --> 00:36:24.000
So we have about 15
regions worldwide.

00:36:24.000 --> 00:36:25.750
As we talked about,
that regional storage,

00:36:25.750 --> 00:36:28.030
or using Nearline and Coldline
in regional locations.

00:36:28.030 --> 00:36:31.390
You can select that region
and store your data in one

00:36:31.390 --> 00:36:32.800
of those specific locations.

00:36:32.800 --> 00:36:34.720
And we're constantly
shipping new regions.

00:36:34.720 --> 00:36:36.880
So just last week we
launched two new regions.

00:36:36.880 --> 00:36:39.589
We launched the Netherlands
region and Montreal region.

00:36:39.589 --> 00:36:41.630
And we're constantly adding
more and more regions

00:36:41.630 --> 00:36:43.840
to Google Cloud
Platform and GCS.

00:36:43.840 --> 00:36:45.377
And if you select
the region, we'll

00:36:45.377 --> 00:36:46.960
keep data in that
region, and that way

00:36:46.960 --> 00:36:50.530
you can have highest performance
between compute and storage.

00:36:50.530 --> 00:36:53.320
And on top of that, we also have
three multi-regional locations

00:36:53.320 --> 00:36:55.870
where we take care of the
geo-redundancy and replicating

00:36:55.870 --> 00:36:58.300
data across multiple regions
within that multi-regional

00:36:58.300 --> 00:36:59.622
location.

00:36:59.622 --> 00:37:01.956
All right, fourth question--
this is a question for you.

00:37:01.956 --> 00:37:03.372
CHRIS TALBOTT: So
how long does it

00:37:03.372 --> 00:37:05.440
take to transfer and load
data using appliances?

00:37:05.440 --> 00:37:07.445
It's faster than sending
data over the wire.

00:37:07.445 --> 00:37:10.280
So I'll take the latter
half of that question first.

00:37:10.280 --> 00:37:12.340
So you saw that very
multicolored graph

00:37:12.340 --> 00:37:13.090
that I showed.

00:37:13.090 --> 00:37:16.075
You could find it
on Cloud.google.com

00:37:16.075 --> 00:37:17.770
/TransferAppliance as well.

00:37:17.770 --> 00:37:18.940
Basically, it depends.

00:37:18.940 --> 00:37:21.250
It depends on your network
situation and how much data

00:37:21.250 --> 00:37:22.090
you have.

00:37:22.090 --> 00:37:25.240
What we've found is that
typically organizations

00:37:25.240 --> 00:37:30.520
have a couple-hundred megabit
type network connection,

00:37:30.520 --> 00:37:33.470
and then they have
multiple petabytes of data.

00:37:33.470 --> 00:37:35.660
So if you're in that
sort of situation,

00:37:35.660 --> 00:37:38.240
then Transfer Appliance
can make a lot of sense.

00:37:38.240 --> 00:37:41.050
Now, dependent on whether or not
you have less data, or perhaps

00:37:41.050 --> 00:37:43.080
a faster connection,
it might make sense

00:37:43.080 --> 00:37:46.000
to upload data over
the wire instead.

00:37:46.000 --> 00:37:48.660
So typically, we have two
options for Transfer Appliance.

00:37:48.660 --> 00:37:51.850
We have a 100 terabyte option,
and we have a 480 terabyte

00:37:51.850 --> 00:37:52.720
option.

00:37:52.720 --> 00:37:55.170
Let's take the large
one as an example.

00:37:55.170 --> 00:37:57.560
It could store upwards
of a petabyte of data.

00:37:57.560 --> 00:38:00.220
It takes a while to load
that onto Appliance.

00:38:00.220 --> 00:38:02.680
It takes a while to
ship it, also, to you,

00:38:02.680 --> 00:38:03.830
and ship it back to us.

00:38:03.830 --> 00:38:06.599
So it takes somewhere between
a month and two months.

00:38:06.599 --> 00:38:08.890
So you got to check out what
your network situation is,

00:38:08.890 --> 00:38:12.179
and it might make sense to use
the Appliance, it might not.

00:38:12.179 --> 00:38:13.220
KIRILL TROPIN: All right.

00:38:13.220 --> 00:38:16.840
So next question,
is GCP Storage--

00:38:16.840 --> 00:38:20.080
GCS-- faster than
storage on other clouds?

00:38:20.080 --> 00:38:21.700
So thinking on
this question, you

00:38:21.700 --> 00:38:23.290
could look back to
our architecture

00:38:23.290 --> 00:38:26.700
and all the advances that Google
has made with our network.

00:38:26.700 --> 00:38:29.350
So GCS typically would be faster
than other cloud providers

00:38:29.350 --> 00:38:32.050
for larger objects, or when
you're serving the content

00:38:32.050 --> 00:38:33.077
outside of our network.

00:38:33.077 --> 00:38:35.160
Because in GCS, we're
carrying data on our network

00:38:35.160 --> 00:38:36.730
for as long as possible.

00:38:36.730 --> 00:38:37.939
It's built in out of the box.

00:38:37.939 --> 00:38:39.271
You don't need to tune anything.

00:38:39.271 --> 00:38:40.938
You don't need to
draw any acceleration.

00:38:40.938 --> 00:38:43.021
You just start using GCS,
we will be carrying data

00:38:43.021 --> 00:38:43.750
on our network.

00:38:43.750 --> 00:38:47.080
So typically, you'll see GCS be
faster than options out there.

00:38:47.080 --> 00:38:49.330
For larger objects, or for
when you're serving data

00:38:49.330 --> 00:38:51.160
outside of our
network, and similarly

00:38:51.160 --> 00:38:53.212
with edge-caching--
because by default

00:38:53.212 --> 00:38:55.420
we have that caching in our
regions around the world.

00:38:55.420 --> 00:38:57.003
If you make your
objects public, we'll

00:38:57.003 --> 00:38:59.020
take them farther out
from our data centers

00:38:59.020 --> 00:39:01.200
in point of presence
around the world.

00:39:01.200 --> 00:39:02.950
In those use cases,
we'll also typically

00:39:02.950 --> 00:39:04.300
be faster than cloud
providers, because we're

00:39:04.300 --> 00:39:05.890
serving data from
the edge, and you

00:39:05.890 --> 00:39:08.710
didn't have to turn on
any CDNs, or integrate,

00:39:08.710 --> 00:39:10.015
or doing any other extra work.

00:39:10.015 --> 00:39:12.967
It just works out of the box
that fast with caching data

00:39:12.967 --> 00:39:14.490
on the edge.

00:39:14.490 --> 00:39:16.610
And then, last
question, can you speak

00:39:16.610 --> 00:39:20.930
to ratio of HDD versus SSD
for different services--

00:39:20.930 --> 00:39:22.950
also, different workloads?

00:39:22.950 --> 00:39:24.540
Yeah, so I don't
think I can directly

00:39:24.540 --> 00:39:25.920
answer about our ratio.

00:39:25.920 --> 00:39:26.970
I should.

00:39:26.970 --> 00:39:30.244
But if you look at our pricing,
you can see the pricing is

00:39:30.244 --> 00:39:32.160
pretty much HDD-based,
because it's so cheap--

00:39:32.160 --> 00:39:34.465
$0.007 and $0.026
per gigabyte month.

00:39:34.465 --> 00:39:36.090
So you can assume
that most of the data

00:39:36.090 --> 00:39:39.550
that you stored on HDD to
achieve that low price.

00:39:39.550 --> 00:39:42.100
But as we optimize
end user performance,

00:39:42.100 --> 00:39:46.104
you will figure that our
metadata is probably stored

00:39:46.104 --> 00:39:47.520
in memory to achieve
highest level

00:39:47.520 --> 00:39:50.034
performance for those
small object operations.

00:39:50.034 --> 00:39:51.450
And then, for
caching as well, you

00:39:51.450 --> 00:39:53.106
can think of different
layers of cache,

00:39:53.106 --> 00:39:54.480
that some things
could be cached.

00:39:54.480 --> 00:39:57.930
So HDD, SSD, and even memory
to achieve that highest

00:39:57.930 --> 00:39:59.800
level of performance
for Hot Data

00:39:59.800 --> 00:40:01.207
and frequently accessed data.

00:40:01.207 --> 00:40:02.040
CHRIS TALBOTT: Yeah.

00:40:02.040 --> 00:40:02.650
KIRILL TROPIN: All right.

00:40:02.650 --> 00:40:04.540
Did you want to add
anything to this?

00:40:04.540 --> 00:40:06.750
CHRIS TALBOTT: No, I think--

00:40:06.750 --> 00:40:07.850
thank you, again.

00:40:07.850 --> 00:40:08.220
KIRILL TROPIN: Yeah.

